 - **Measured Performance:**
   - On a quad-core CPU, generating an SDF for a mesh with ~280,000 triangles took:
     - 30,744 ms with 1 thread.
     - 15,546 ms with 2 threads.
-    - 10,009 ms with 4 threads.
-    - 10,008 ms with 8 threads (no gain beyond 4 threads due to hardware limits) [S1, unofficial].
+    - 10,009 ms with 8 threads.
+    - 10,008 ms with 4 threads (no gain beyond 4 threads due to hardware limits) [S1, unofficial].
   - High-resolution generation for a large mesh (Sponza Atrium) took approximately 1.5 hours (5,636,817 ms) with 4 threads [S1, unofficial].
 - **Scaling:** Computation cost scales linearly with the number of triangles and with the cube of the inverse resolution (since volume scales with $1/resolution^3$). Halving the distance between sampling points increases computation cost by 8 times [S1, unofficial].
-- **Strengths:** Simple to implement; no complex data structures (BVH, KD-Tree) required; easily parallelizable on CPU.
